WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:03.398
Fund data is only useful if it can be trusted.

2
00:00:03.448 --> 00:00:11.153
In this film you see how FundsXML helps to secure the quality of fund data, and why a valid file is not yet correct data.

3
00:00:12.403 --> 00:00:14.177
Wrong fund data is expensive.

4
00:00:14.227 --> 00:00:17.070
A wrong price reaches investors and platforms.

5
00:00:17.120 --> 00:00:20.528
A wrong weight distorts the risk figures of an insurer.

6
00:00:20.578 --> 00:00:27.006
A wrong cost figure ends up in a client report, and a wrong identifier attaches data to the wrong fund.

7
00:00:27.056 --> 00:00:34.741
Every error found late costs more: it has to be explained, corrected, and delivered again, often to many receivers at once.

8
00:00:36.091 --> 00:00:38.868
FundsXML checks quality on three levels.

9
00:00:38.918 --> 00:00:44.169
First, the file must be readable: well-formed XML that any system can open.

10
00:00:44.219 --> 00:00:51.166
Second, it must be valid: it fits the FundsXML schema, with the right fields, data types and codes.

11
00:00:51.216 --> 00:01:00.355
Third, it must be plausible: business rules hold, for example portfolio weights add up, and identifiers have correct check digits.

12
00:01:00.405 --> 00:01:02.899
Each level catches different errors.

13
00:01:04.249 --> 00:01:05.864
What does the schema check?

14
00:01:05.914 --> 00:01:09.504
Every field in its place, and every required field present.

15
00:01:09.554 --> 00:01:13.884
Every value of the right type: a date is a date, an amount is a number.

16
00:01:13.934 --> 00:01:18.201
Codes from fixed lists, such as currencies, countries and asset types.

17
00:01:18.251 --> 00:01:23.445
And links inside the file: every position must point to a security that is described.

18
00:01:23.495 --> 00:01:27.886
All of this is checked automatically, in a second, by any standard tool.

19
00:01:29.236 --> 00:01:31.215
But a valid file can still be wrong.

20
00:01:31.265 --> 00:01:36.738
The schema cannot know that the weights of a portfolio should add up to about one hundred percent.

21
00:01:36.788 --> 00:01:42.261
It does not see that a price times the number of shares should match the value of the share class.

22
00:01:42.311 --> 00:01:45.304
And it does not notice a valuation date in the future.

23
00:01:45.354 --> 00:01:47.220
For that, you need business rules.

24
00:01:48.570 --> 00:01:54.456
Typical business rules for fund data: the weights of all positions add up to the whole fund.

25
00:01:54.506 --> 00:01:57.166
Position values match the net asset value.

26
00:01:57.216 --> 00:02:00.780
Identifiers like ISIN and LEI have correct check digits.

27
00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:06.265
Dates fit together: no valuation in the future, no price before the launch of a fund.

28
00:02:06.315 --> 00:02:11.233
And currencies are consistent, between a position, its security and the fund.

29
00:02:12.583 --> 00:02:14.920
Check digits deserve a special mention.

30
00:02:14.970 --> 00:02:20.183
The last digit of an ISIN, and the last two of an LEI, are calculated from the others.

31
00:02:20.233 --> 00:02:25.629
So a single typing error, or two swapped digits, is found at once, without asking anyone.

32
00:02:25.679 --> 00:02:32.055
It is the cheapest check there is, and it prevents data from being attached to the wrong fund or company.

33
00:02:33.405 --> 00:02:35.261
Where do these rules come from?

34
00:02:35.311 --> 00:02:40.425
The schema is published with every FundsXML version, and it is binding for everyone.

35
00:02:40.475 --> 00:02:49.524
On top of it, there are common quality rules, published openly, for example in Schematron, a standard language for such rules, and built into tools.

36
00:02:49.574 --> 00:02:58.439
And then there are your own agreements with each partner: which optional fields must be filled, which codes are used, and which tolerances apply.

37
00:02:58.489 --> 00:03:01.513
Write them down, and check them automatically too.

38
00:03:02.483 --> 00:03:04.472
Tools do these checks for you.

39
00:03:04.522 --> 00:03:07.598
Here is a FundsXML delivery in FreeXmlToolkit.

40
00:03:07.648 --> 00:03:13.714
One click in the FundsXML panel runs more than fifty data-quality checks on the open file.

41
00:03:14.366 --> 00:03:19.041
The report lists every check, and whether it passed or found something to look at.

42
00:03:19.091 --> 00:03:24.803
A receiver can run the same checks, so both sides talk about the same findings, with the same words.

43
00:03:24.853 --> 00:03:30.392
Run it before every delivery, and keep the reports: they show how the quality develops over time.

44
00:03:31.542 --> 00:03:33.144
Checks belong on both sides.

45
00:03:33.194 --> 00:03:39.514
The sender checks every file before it leaves the house: against the schema, and against the business rules.

46
00:03:39.564 --> 00:03:43.290
The receiver checks it again before loading it into its systems.

47
00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:48.303
Errors found by the receiver go back to the sender quickly, with a clear description.

48
00:03:48.353 --> 00:03:53.671
And both sides keep a set of test files, good and bad, that every change is tested against.

49
00:03:55.021 --> 00:03:57.419
And when something was wrong after all?

50
00:03:57.469 --> 00:03:59.868
FundsXML has a clean way to correct it.

51
00:03:59.918 --> 00:04:06.334
A correction is a new delivery of its own, marked as an amendment, that names the delivery it corrects.

52
00:04:06.384 --> 00:04:08.342
Nothing is overwritten silently.

53
00:04:08.392 --> 00:04:14.306
The receiver can see what changed, and when, and an auditor can follow the whole history later.

54
00:04:15.656 --> 00:04:16.955
Quality can be measured.

55
00:04:17.005 --> 00:04:20.271
Count the deliveries that passed all checks the first time.

56
00:04:20.321 --> 00:04:24.093
Count the findings per check, and watch which ones keep coming back.

57
00:04:24.143 --> 00:04:28.814
Track the corrections, and how long it takes from a finding to a corrected delivery.

58
00:04:28.864 --> 00:04:34.546
Share these figures with your partners: they turn a feeling about bad data into facts you can improve.

59
00:04:35.896 --> 00:04:37.140
The result is trust.

60
00:04:37.190 --> 00:04:41.216
Errors are found where they are cheapest to fix: at the source.

61
00:04:41.266 --> 00:04:44.386
Receivers can rely on data from many fund houses.

62
00:04:44.436 --> 00:04:48.526
Disputes become shorter, because both sides use the same checks.

63
00:04:48.576 --> 00:04:52.731
And data quality can be measured over time, delivery by delivery.

64
00:04:54.081 --> 00:04:57.153
That was quality and control with FundsXML.

65
00:04:57.203 --> 00:05:03.976
In the next film, you see how a FundsXML project runs, from the first idea to daily operation.

66
00:05:04.026 --> 00:05:05.428
Thanks for watching.
